mathematical-mirror-01910fe9.interpretation:
In this equation, 

\Psi_{total}
 represents the overall or combined state of a system. The term 

\Psi_{ret}
 signifies the 'retarded' component of the state, which typically relates to the system's response to past events or causes propagating forward in time. Conversely, 

\Psi_{adv}
 represents the 'advanced' component, which could be interpreted as the system's influence on future events or causes propagating backward in time. The factor of 

$\frac{1}{2}$
 implies that both the retarded and advanced components contribute equally to the total state. This averaging suggests a balance or symmetry between the temporal influences, possibly arising in scenarios where causality is not strictly unidirectional or when considering time-symmetric formulations.

mathematical-mirror-01910fe9.limits_and_boundary:
As 

\Psi_{ret}
 approaches 

\Psi_{adv}
, 

\Psi_{total}
 will converge to that common value. If 

\Psi_{ret}
 and 

\Psi_{adv}
 represent different types of information or energy, and one dominates significantly over the other, 

\Psi_{total}
 will be primarily characterized by the dominant term. For instance, if 

\Psi_{ret} $\to$ 0
, then 

\Psi_{total} $\to$ $\frac{1}{2}$

\Psi_{adv}
, and vice versa.

current-orbital-electron.interpretation:
The formula for the current 
 (
I
) due to an orbiting electron is given by 
 (
I = ev/(2̇ r)
). Here, 
 (
e
) is the elementary charge of the electron, which is a fundamental constant representing the magnitude of the charge of a single electron (approximately 1.602 x 10^-19 Coulombs). 
 (
v
) is the orbital speed of the electron, measured in meters per second (m/s). This is the linear velocity at which the electron traverses its orbit. 
 (
r
) is the radius of the electron's orbit, measured in meters (m). The term 
 (
2̇ r
) represents the circumference of the circular orbit. The current 
 (
I
) is expressed in Amperes (A), where 1 Ampere is equal to 1 Coulomb per second (C/s). Fundamentally, current is the amount of charge passing a point per unit time. In this context, the charge 
 (
e
) completes one full orbit (a distance of 
 (
2̇ r
)) in a time period 
 (
T = 2̇ r/v
), so the current is 
 (
I = q/T = e/(2̇ r/v) = ev/(2̇ r)
).

current-orbital-electron.symmetry_origin:
This formula is derived from the definition of electric current. Current (
I
) is defined as the rate of flow of charge (
I = Δq/Δt
). In this case, the total charge passing any point in the orbit during one period of revolution (
Δt = T
) is the charge of the electron (
Δq = e
). The period of revolution (
T
) for an object moving with constant speed (
v
) in a circular path of radius (
r
) is given by the circumference (
2̇ r
) divided by the speed (
v
), so 
 (
T = 2̇ r/v
). Substituting these into the definition of current yields 
 (
I = e/(2̇ r/v) = ev/(2̇ r)
). The derivation implicitly assumes a classical, circular orbit, which is a simplification of quantum mechanical reality.

